        <body>
            <div xml:id="d476">

                <pb facs="0441" n="325" sameAs="frus:frus1914/pg325"/>

                <note rend="inline" type="source">File No. 811.7111/64.</note>
                <head ana="#head_untitled-inferred-from-preceding-sibling-head">[Untitled]</head>
                <opener>
                    <seg rendition="#float-left">No. 81.]</seg>
                    <dateline rendition="#float-right">
                        <placeName>
                            <hi rendition="#small-caps">British Embassy</hi>, <lb/>
                            <hi rendition="#italic">Washington</hi></placeName>, <date
                            calendar="gregorian" when="1914-03-17">
                            <hi rendition="#italic">March 17, 1914</hi></date>.</dateline>
                </opener>
                <p>
                    <hi rendition="#small-caps">Sir</hi>: With reference to my Note No. 75 of the
                    12th instant relative to “Chief Sam’s” plan for promoting negro emigration to
                    the Gold Coast Colony I have the honour to inform you, under instructions from
                    Sir Edward Grey, that the ordinance in question is being passed.</p>
                <p>In this connection I may add that the British Vice Consul at Portland, Maine, has
                    been instructed not to issue British papers to the steamer <hi
                        rendition="#italic">Curityba</hi> which I understand has been purchased by
                    “Chief Sam” and is the property of the Akim Trading Company, of South
                    Dakota.</p>
                <p>I have [etc.]</p>
                <closer>
                    <signed>
                        <hi rendition="#small-caps">Cecil Spring Rice</hi></signed>.</closer>
            </div>
        </body>
    </text>
